@startuml wire-probe
' Is a wire physically there? Grounded in Src/wire_probe.c and in the
' adapter in Src/bringup.c that gives it the real pin read.

title Two readings of one pin, and a comparison of two resistors

skinparam shadowing false

start

note right
  **The question no protocol can ask.**
  A bus only speaks once the wiring works.
  When nothing answers, a dead device, a
  device at another address, a device in
  SPI mode and a jumper in the hole next
  door all look the same from inside the
  protocol. Four repairs behind one word.
end note

:read the pin with NO pull;
note right
  Reported, never the verdict. A pin left
  alone shows whatever charge is still on
  it, which is why a jumper that reaches
  nothing so often reads high.
  Read FIRST: after a pull-down it would
  show the pull-down instead of the line.
end note

:read the same pin while the chip pulls it DOWN;

if (still high?) then (yes)
  :**WIRE_HELD**;
  note right
    Something outside wins. The module's
    own pull-up is about 4.7k, the chip's
    pull-down about 40k, ten times weaker.
    So the wire reaches this pin AND the
    thing at the far end has power.
  end note
  stop
else (no)
  :**WIRE_LOOSE**;
  note right
    Nothing outside. The wire goes
    somewhere else, or is broken, or the
    module has no supply.
  end note
  stop
endif

legend bottom
  The deciding, the two digit report and the sweep are pure logic in
  wire_probe.c, tested on a PC against a fake that REMEMBERS: a pull-down
  drains a loose line, so reading in the wrong order gives the wrong answer.
  59 checks, 9 mutants. A stateless fake let the ordering mutant survive.
  Only the pin read itself is on the board, in bringup.c, and it costs
  nothing in a shipping image because both files sit behind one switch.
endlegend

@enduml
